Choosing a versioning strategy is only half the problem. **How you retire old versions is where most teams fail.** Without a clear deprecation timeline, clients never migrate — they stay on v1 forever because "it still works," and you're maintaining two code paths indefinitely.

The IETF `Sunset` header (RFC 8594) is the standard mechanism for communicating deprecation dates. Adding `Sunset: Sat, 1 Jan 2027 00:00:00 GMT` to every v1 response tells clients — and monitoring tools — exactly when the version goes away. Pair it with `Deprecation: true` and a `Link` header pointing to the migration guide:

```java
@GetMapping("/api/v1/users/{id}")
public ResponseEntity<UserV1Response> getUserV1(@PathVariable Long id,
        HttpServletResponse response) {
    // Deprecation headers on every v1 response — monitoring alerts on these
    response.setHeader("Deprecation", "true");
    response.setHeader("Sunset", "Sat, 01 Jan 2027 00:00:00 GMT");
    response.setHeader("Link", "</api/v2/users/{id}>; rel=\"successor-version\"");
    return ResponseEntity.ok(userService.findByIdV1(id));
}
```

**Stripe's version pinning approach is the most client-friendly model.** Each API key is created with a version — whatever version was current at creation time. New API behavior ships as additive changes to all versions. Only breaking changes require a new version, and clients are pinned to their creation-date version forever unless they explicitly opt in to the new version. This eliminates forced migrations and gives clients a safe, predictable environment. The tradeoff is infrastructure complexity — the server must maintain backward-compatible behavior across many versions simultaneously.

For semantic versioning vs date-based: **semver (v1, v2, v3) is clearer for public APIs** where you want to signal the magnitude of changes. Date-based versioning (`2024-01-15`) is better for APIs that change frequently and want to provide granular rollback points without major version theatre.
